{"type": "tool_result", "name": "read_file", "output": "{\"content\": \"1|# QG Engineering Decisions\\n2|\\n3|Approved baseline architectural decisions recorded from `DESIGN.md`. These are established by the authoritative design specification, not invented during bootstrap. Implementation-level choices for APIs/classes that do not yet exist are deliberately **not** recorded here; they are made when the relevant prompt is executed and validated.\\n4|\\n5|## DEC-001 — Native C++20 Application\\n6|\\n7|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n8|\\n9|Decision:\\n10|QG is a native compiled C++20 application. No scripting runtime, no managed/browser-hosted application runtime.\\n11|\\n12|Source:\\n13|DESIGN.md §0 (implementation baseline), §15.5 (build and dependency contract).\\n14|\\n15|Rationale:\\n16|The product requires direct libmpv Render API integration, Qt Quick scene-graph/OpenGL rendering, POSIX/GIO filesystem access, and bounded multi-threaded performance — all of which are served by a native compiled stack.\\n17|\\n18|Affected systems:\\n19|Entire application; build system; packaging.\\n20|\\n21|## DEC-002 — Qt 6 Quick/QML Presentation Layer\\n22|\\n23|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n24|\\n25|Decision:\\n26|The UI is implemented in Qt 6 (Qt Quick / QML + Qt Quick Controls 2). QML owns presentation only (layout, transitions, visual state, delegates, interactions, animations).\\n27|\\n28|Source:\\n29|DESIGN.md §3.4, §14.1, §15.1.\\n30|\\n31|Rationale:\\n32|Qt Quick provides a hardware-accelerated declarative presentation layer that composes with the same OpenGL pipeline used by the video surface, while keeping domain logic in C++.\\n33|\\n34|Affected systems:\\n35|All UI; QML module registration; theme.\\n36|\\n37|## DEC-003 — C++ Ownership of Domain Logic\\n38|\\n39|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n40|\\n41|Decision:\\n42|Authoritative application behavior lives in C++ (models, controllers, services, workers, persistence, playback). QML must not own filesystem, persistence, playback, sorting, or search state.\\n43|\\n44|Source:\\n45|DESIGN.md §14.1, §15.1; `.hermes.md` Presentation/Domain Boundary.\\n46|\\n47|Rationale:\\n48|High-volume data and stateful logic belong in typed, testable C++ with clear ownership; QML JS is not an authoritative state store.\\n49|\\n50|Affected systems:\\n51|All controllers, models, and services.\\n52|\\n53|## DEC-004 — Ubuntu/Linux Desktop Target\\n54|\\n55|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n56|\\n57|Decision:\\n58|QG targets Ubuntu/Linux desktop, using POSIX filesystem APIs, native desktop conventions, and XDG base directories.\\n59|\\n60|Source:\\n61|DESIGN.md header (platform), §16.1, §25.12.\\n62|\\n63|Rationale:\\n64|The product is a Linux video file explorer whose core value depends on real filesystem, mounted-volume, Trash, and desktop-application integration.\\n65|\\n66|Affected systems:\\n67|Filesystem, mounts, Trash, packaging, XDG paths.\\n68|\\n69|## DEC-005 — X11/xcb Primary Runtime\\n70|\\n71|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n72|\\n73|Decision:\\n74|The primary runtime target is Ubuntu on X11 via Qt's `xcb` platform plugin. Wayland portability is retained where inexpensive but must not weaken the X11-first implementation.\\n75|\\n76|Source:\\n77|DESIGN.md §19.3, §25.12, §16.1.\\n78|\\n79|Rationale:\\n80|X11/xcb is the acceptance target; the Render-API surface is used identically on Wayland, so no X11-specific playback code is required.\\n81|\\n82|Affected systems:\\n83|Rendering, playback surface, windowing.\\n84|\\n85|## DEC-006 — OpenGL Qt Quick Rendering Backend\\n86|\\n87|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n88|\\n89|Decision:\\n90|Qt Quick is forced to the OpenGL scene-graph backend on Linux before the first `QQuickWindow` is created, so libmpv's OpenGL Render API and QG's UI share one accelerated rendering pipeline.\\n91|\\n92|Source:\\n93|DESIGN.md §15.1, §19.3, §16.1.\\n94|\\n95|Rationale:\\n96|Sharing one OpenGL pipeline lets mpv frames compose directly into the Qt Quick scene graph with no foreign child window.\\n97|\\n98|Affected systems:\\n99|`main.cpp`, playback surface, scene graph.\\n100|\\n101|## DEC-007 — libmpv C API for Playback\\n102|\\n103|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n104|\\n105|Decision:\\n106|Playback uses the libmpv C API. `playback/mpv_backend.cpp` is the only translation unit allowed to create/destroy the mpv handle or call libmpv.\\n107|\\n108|Source:\\n109|DESIGN.md §19.1, §15.1, §15.5.\\n110|\\n111|Rationale:\\n112|Centralized ownership of the mpv handle yields deterministic teardown and prevents scattered/duplicate players.\\n113|\\n114|Affected systems:\\n115|Playback backend, lifecycle, shutdown.\\n116|\\n117|## DEC-008 — One Active Heavy Playback Session\\n118|\\n119|Status: APPROVED BASELINE\\n120|\\n121|Decision:\\n122|Exactly one mpv instance and one active playback session exist at any time. Starting new playback reuses the same handle via `loadfile`, never a second handle.\\n123|\\n124|Source:\\n125|DESIGN.md §19.2, §1.5.\\n126|\\n127|Rationale:\\n128|Bounded memory/GPU load, instant switching, and a single authoritative playback state. The product explicitly accepts the tradeoff of no simultaneous dual playback.\\n129|\\n130|Affected systems:\\n131...", "duration_ms": 7, "is_error": false, "timestamp": 1791453319891}
